        {
          "kind": "guide",
          "title": "Received provider guide",
          "pages": [
            {
              "title": "1 \u00b7 Received interface / domains",
              "text": "Projection Office v2. This complete guide applies to both packets. Source coordinates are artwork pixels, x right and y down. Wall coordinates are plan units, x right and y down, within the declared wall width and height. Inline panel crop and placement are retained survey envelopes, NOT v2 registration correspondences. The registration-report attachment is authoritative. Select a panel by its panelId, not by list position. Artwork polygons are painted in listed order."
            },
            {
              "title": "2 \u00b7 Crop and local orientation",
              "text": "For each registration panel, sourceWindow is [x,y,width,height] in source pixels. Discard source content outside that closed rectangle before projecting. Normalize the window to (u,v) in [0,1]\u00b2. If reflectU is true, first replace u by 1-u. Next apply quarterTurnsClockwise times (u,v) -> (1-v,u). This oriented local square is the control report local coordinate domain. Do not rotate the wall or the display aperture."
            },
            {
              "title": "3 \u00b7 Registration / homogeneous division",
              "text": "Each control matches local [u,v] to station [east,north]. Controls are samples, NOT ordered corners; pair values from the same row. Fit the unique nonsingular projective homography from these four pairs. Divide the first and second homogeneous components by the third. Convert station to wall with x=(east-originEast)/stationUnitsPerWallUnit, y=wallHeight-(north-originNorth)/stationUnitsPerWallUnit, using the report stationFrame. A projective mapping, not a bilinear or affine warp, is required. Station frame conversion and source orientation also apply to inverse edits."
            },
            {
              "title": "4 \u00b7 Visible region and seam",
              "text": "Clip projected artwork to panel.displayPolygon (closed, convex) and subtract all panel.obstructions, including their boundaries. These wall-space polygons do not rotate with the artwork. A source pin is valid only inside the sourceWindow and its own panel visible region. At a shared boundary, choose the first valid panel in report seamPriority. Away from overlap, the unique visible panel owns the wall point. Pin validity also requires an inverse point inside that panel sourceWindow. The gray obstruction is unpainted; off-wall space is unpainted."
            },
            {
              "title": "5 \u00b7 Pin edits and saved plans",
              "text": "Wall numeric placement and wall taps move the selected pin through the full inverse transform and update its panel ownership. An invalid point (obstruction, outside display, or outside the inverse source window) is rejected: leave panel, source and selected mark unchanged, and show a rejection message. Source numeric placement stays on the selected panel and obeys the same visibility rule; only the seam owner is valid on a shared boundary. Display source and wall coordinates to two decimals. Internal precision is retained. A selected pin is a black circle with a white center; all pins use this mark, selected adds a purple outer ring. Marks are not part of the artwork."
            },
            {
              "title": "6 \u00b7 Editing / receipt serialization",
              "text": "Undo reverses the last accepted move; a rejected move does not add history. Cancel restores the last saved plan for the current packet (or its received pins if never saved). Save plan persists source-domain pins with panel IDs, label and note independently per packet, and emits a readable plan receipt. Receipt rows include panelId, source [x,y], local [u,v] AFTER orientation, and station [east,north] obtained from the registration. Reopen saved and an ordinary reload restore that saved plan. Switching packets never overwrites a saved plan. A rebuild explicitly resets the imported scene. Received guide, raw report, artwork and intended projection plates are always readable, including before a repair."
            }
          ]
        },
